MODEL EVALUATION PLOTS: A QUICK REFERENCE GUIDE
-----------------------------------------------
This document provides a summary of the four primary diagnostic plots used to evaluate the performance of the regression model predicting Red Blood Cell (RBC) properties.

1. PERCENTAGE ERROR BAR CHART
-----------------------------------------------
Function: Provides a high-level summary of the model's accuracy and stability across all four predicted properties (diameter, thickness, ratio, refractive index).

How to Read it:
- Blue Bar (Avg |Error|): Represents the Mean Absolute Percentage Error. A lower bar indicates better overall accuracy.
- Orange Bar (Max |Error|): Represents the worst single prediction the model made across the entire test dataset.
- X-Axis Labels (+/- Std Dev): The value in the parenthesis under the property name is the Standard Deviation of the error. A small standard deviation means the model is highly consistent; a large one means the error fluctuates wildly from image to image.


2. TRUE VS. PREDICTED SCATTER PLOT
-----------------------------------------------
Function: Visualizes the direct correlation between the ground truth (actual values) and the model's predictions. It is the best tool for spotting systematic biases.

How to Read it:
- The Red Dashed Line (y = x): This is the "Line of Perfect Agreement". If the model were 100% accurate, every single prediction would land exactly on this line.
- The Dots: Each dot represents one tested image.

Interpretation:
- Dots tightly hugging the red line indicate high accuracy.
- If a cluster of dots sits consistently above the red line, the model is systematically overestimating that property.
- If dots sit consistently below the line, the model is underestimating.
- Dots floating far away from the line are your outliers.


3. ERROR DISTRIBUTION BOXPLOT
-----------------------------------------------
Function: Displays the statistical spread of the percentage errors, highlighting the median and isolating extreme outliers.

How to Read it:
- The Red Line (Median): The middle value of all errors. Half the images had a lower error than this, and half had a higher error.
- The Blue Box (Interquartile Range - IQR): Contains the middle 50% of your data. A narrow box means most predictions have very similar error rates.
- The Whiskers (Black Lines): Extend to show the range of the "normal" data distribution.
- Standalone Dots (Fliers): These are the outliers. If your max error is 9.12% but the box ends at 2%, that 9.12% will appear as a lonely dot at the top, proving it is a rare exception rather than the rule.


4. BLAND-ALTMAN PLOT
-----------------------------------------------
Function: A staple in medical and biophysical statistics. Instead of just looking at accuracy, it evaluates whether the magnitude of the error changes depending on the size of the actual measurement.

How to Read it:
- X-Axis: The average of the True and Predicted values (representing the estimated true size/value of the property).
- Y-Axis: The Difference (Predicted minus True). A perfect prediction sits at exactly 0 on the Y-axis.
- Solid Red Line (Mean Difference): Shows the overall bias. If this line is at +0.5, the model overpredicts by 0.5 units on average.
- Dashed Gray Lines (Limits of Agreement): Represent +/- 1.96 Standard Deviations. Statistically, 95% of all errors should fall between these two lines.

Interpretation:
- Look for patterns. If the dots spread out into a "fan" shape or a slope as you move to the right along the X-axis, it means the model becomes less accurate when the property (like diameter or refractive index) gets larger. Ideally, dots should look like a random cloud scattered evenly around the red line.